[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue with one fix queued. Read the bodies, not the subjects,
and it holds three problems. The parser fix covers only the first. The other two stay broken after
it ships, and #4471 would close over them.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one issue. Three tickets, one per problem below. Each report counts once, in one of them.
</Callout>

## The split

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

## The three problems

**`parser`: the shared parser reads dates month first.** Every tenant on file writes the day
first. A booking for 7 April goes out as 4 July. This is the fix #4471 already queued.

**`hint`: booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field.** At the tenants on booking-v3,
people type what the hint shows, month first, and the form reads it day first. Only reports through
the form, only at those tenants. The parser fix does not touch it: the hint has to change.

**`one-day`: the date moves by one day, it does not swap.** "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in
Toronto got Monday." The desk already read these five as a one-day move
(`desk-notes.json`), but the table on #4471 still counts them under `parser`. Day and month are
not swapped in any of them, so the parser fix will not change them. It looks like a time-zone
conversion, and it needs its own owner.

## Why they were merged

[Triage](/kb/triage/dedupe) merges on the subject line alone
([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)), and every report here had a subject about
a wrong date. That page has not been re-read since 2024. Intake's tags did not help either: they
disagree with the tenant's cause on dozens of reports. Read a report's tenant, channel and body
before you merge it.

## What this page does not change

#4471 and decision 0001 stay as they are. Splitting the ticket and replacing the decision is the
desk's call; this page gives the counts for it.

